{
  "case_id": "2010-09-115",
  "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In 1986, Congress converted what had been before that time a merely peripheral concern of immigration policy -- that is, how to regulate worker authorization -- and converted it into a core concern of immigration policy by the passage of the -- of the Immigration Reform and Control Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why -- why is that a problem if, as -- as the Federal statute requires and the State statutes require, you have to show an intent to hire an unauthorized worker? Isn't -- isn't that what the State statutes here require?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nearly a quarter of a century ago, Congress declared Federal employer sanctions central, not peripheral, to the policy of immigration law. Congress broadly swept away State and local laws, pre-empting any sanction upon those who employ unauthorized aliens, with the sole exception being a mere parenthetical for licensing and similar laws."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Katyal",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We think a licensing law, as Congress defined it in IRCA, was the traditional licensing laws that were in place in 1986. Those were largely farm labor contractor laws. They were aimed at fitness to do business, and they had a few essential characteristics in those laws. They -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Suppose I have a licensing law which says if you do this, your license will be revoked. Does that remain a licensing law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I'm -- doesn't it frustrate the congressional intent when the Federal law says that the I-9 can be used for no purpose other than the Federal adjudication of whether a violation has occurred or not? Doesn't it frustrate that law to have the States raise a defense that depends on forcing someone to disclose something that the Federal law protects?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. O'Grady",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We think the -- that question is answered by looking at -- into the conflict pre-emption analysis, because Congress didn't address the role of the States with regard to E-Verify. And we aren't -\u00ad"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Do -- do you make it mandatory?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State of Arizona",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. O'GRADY: Well, our statute says you shall use E-Verify. We don't impose a penalty against employers who fail to use it. The consequences are the same as they are under Federal law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: If they fail to do it, then they cannot receive any, quote, \"grant, loan, or performance-based incentive,\" end quote, from the State. That's what the law says, isn't it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Phillips",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But remember, Congress was balancing three, at least, very difficult problems: minimizing burdens on the employers, minimizing discrimination against people who are permitted to be hired, and avoiding hiring people"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0003-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if a -- an employer is convicted of -- of violating IRCA and a criminal sanction, and he happens to be a barber, and the State licensing law says if you're convicted of a -\u00ad of a Federal crime you will lose your license, it is available to the State under those circumstances"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Phillips",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "32e8c22bc0e45e925c75b29cd5725f46c7eec3c75ac79c5e0616fe94c59e4f41",
      "evidence_id": "ev-32e8c22bc0e4-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, if you -- if you interpret the savings clause as I do, which means truly as a supplement to Federal -- Federal adjudication, then it is a very narrow limitation on that basis, because at that point you've already invoked the entirety of the Federal scheme, and it doesn't modify the balance on those broader legal issues"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-115",
      "confidence": 0.0,
